eating  my patient so we are now sharing data  between these three institutions and it  includes data points like age and  diagnosis and genomics genomic variants  and treatments etc and the data that is  shared is also the data that you can  view from other institutions this is  called the oncology precision network  it's been a very exciting effort in  addition to these three founding  institutions we have numerous additional  institutions that are joining as we  speak including integrated health  delivery networks across the country and  several academic institutions that are  eager to join in share data so that  their own single institution set of data  can be enlarged by seeing other  institutions data this oncology  precision network was highlighted by  Vice President Biden at his cancer  moonshot event in June at Brown  University the way this works is that as  institutions generate genomic data all  that is consolidated through our science  software platform and is aggregated into  one common database and then we can run  analytics and  ask powerful questions such as show me  all of the patients that have as  represented in this slide as particular  tumor type or maybe I would like to  search the common database by genes or  specific alterations and I can see what  types of treatments those patients had  and so this oncology precision network  or open is becoming a very powerful  resource to understand what types of  alterations exist amongst patients  across the country and what types of  treatments are yielding responses if  there's anyone on the webinar that would  be interested in learning more or even  possibly contributing data to the  oncology precision network please don't  hesitate to reach out to me directly and  I can help answer any questions I'll  transition now to a second project that  we are working on the Intermountain  Healthcare biorepository consists of  approximately 4 million clinical  specimens that have been collected since  1975 these samples are archived in FFPE  blocks over the last 40 years  and what's extraordinarily unusual about  these is that we have clinical digital  and searchable outcomes in our  electronic data warehouse over about 30  years what that practically means is I  can walk into the bio repository and  pictured there on the slide is an actual  picture of our by repository I can open  one of those boxes pull out a shelf pull  a specimen out of it and then I could go  into the electronic data warehouse and  find all of the digital outcomes  associated with that specimen over a  30-year period represents an  extraordinarily powerful opportunity to  ask important questions in order to  unlock the secrets hidden away in those  specimens from the last 40 years we are  building a high-throughput sequencing  facility on the platform of an x10  Illumina HiSeq instrument that will  allow us the capacity to do up to  10,000 genomes a year and it will enable  the sequencing of our by repository for  novel discovery and as we take those  genomic alterations and combine them  with the clinical outcomes for each  sample we'll be able to discover for  example the molecular fingerprint that  may predict for recurrence in colon  cancer for predictors of response to  certain treatments in breast cancer and  on and on the list of possibilities and  questions goes finely and in conclusion  we have begun to recognize that one of  our great strengths is the delivery of  medicine in the clinical setting to  become increasingly excellent in that  pursuit we have recognized the need to  develop new and adapt novel technologies  in order to accomplish that we have  recently formed a partnership with the  Stanford genome technology Center where  we will be jointly developing  technologies that have particular  clinical application and then clinically  implementing those including molecular  diagnostics wearable sensor devices and  others so in summary what we have found  in our efforts at Intermountain  Healthcare and our precision medicine  program is that in fact precision cancer  medicine is feasible and you know as we  applied it we found that there is  actually an improved progression-free  survival and so this is significant for  patients it represents another treatment  option for providers and patients alike  sort of when patients become refractory  or if they are not candidates for  standard therapy or sending therapy  hasn't worked that providing a precision  medicine approach remains an outstanding  and viable option for them additional  clinical trials are currently underway  including the National Cancer Institute  match trial and the taper trial  sponsored by ASCO both of which are  evaluating the use of precision targeted  therapies based on genomic test  results to determine whether precision  cancer medicine is indeed associated  with superior outcomes as the UCSD study  has identified in our study as well  additional summary points from our  experience include that healthcare  associated costs for precision medicine  patients is not higher than standard  chemotherapy approaches we've also found  the pediatric tumors higher hide harbor  high numbers clinically actionable  variants and finally that the somatic  sequence sequencing of forced somatic  alterations invariably identify these  germline alterations so I would just  like to pause and acknowledge the very  big team comprised of standing and  capable individuals who have made all of  this work possible and that includes the  list of individuals there from our inner  mount precision genomics team as well as  colleagues from Stanford University some  funding has come from the NCI and from  ASCO for which we are grateful and with  that I think we have a few minutes left  to take some questions and I'd be happy  to answer some questions thank you well  thank you so very much doctor for that  amazing and informative presentation  before we get started on our  question-and-answer session I like 