 biopsy knowing  that either tumor evolution has yielded  new alterations or biopsy from that same  tumor but in a slightly different  location may yield other actual  mutations that we can target with  different drugs so we don't believe it's  possible to address every single  alteration harbored in every tumor from  the outset however by taking different  biopsies at different points in time you  can in fact identify different Actimel  mutations that add to the list of  treatment options for that particular  patient great question  and have you examined any disparities in  access to precision cancer medicine and  more SES populations and Medicare  patients we haven't yet discovered on  geographical disparities in access to  drug but there are disparities amongst  different payers some pairs more readily  approved  targeted therapies based on precision  genomic testing whereas other payers are  very slow or frankly reject those  requests and we have to go through an  appeals process we've also found that  payers are I think becoming increasingly  educated and understanding of this  approach and we're seeing increasingly  frequent approvals for therapies which  is encouraging so that's a great  question I'm glad that was asked and are  you exploring liquid biopsies in  addition to solid tumor biopsy liquid  biopsy is an exciting and potentially  transformative technology in cancer we  like others are exploring the  applications of liquid biopsy we're  doing that in the research setting  currently with patient samples and are  evaluating the application and the  appropriate applications some of the  immediate applications that are most  obvious that our patients will begin  experiencing very soon include liquid  biopsies for monitoring response to  therapy we are finding that tracking the  abundance of certain alterations in the  circulating tumor DNA fraction of course  lends to response to treatment and as we  verify that's true we'll be able to  delay for CT scanning for example if we  see that a patient is responding based  on declining fractions of molecular  variants in the peripheral blood then we  can have confidence in delaying CT scan  because we know they're doing well or  responding additional applications for  liquid biopsy that we're exploring and  others are aware of this as well include  the use of liquid biopsies for  monitoring and surveillance so if a  patient is in remission for example can  we use liquid biopsy to monitor for  disease recurrence and then ultimately  we anticipate being able to use liquid  biopsy to in undiagnosed patients so to  diagnose disease that earlier and  earlier stages of of disease which is  exciting and perhaps the most  revolutionary application of liquid  biopsy technology great question  our next question is somatic alterations  was like some germline alterations by  germline do you mean heredity yeah great  question of course the tumor has or  harbors alterations that have occurred  somatically meaning you know at some  point during the patient's life and by  germline exactly as the questioner  proposed I'm referencing inherited DNA  so the germline DNA is what I mean by  that is the the copies of the  chromosomes and genes that patient may  have inherited from their parents and  has been present with them from birth  and is also the same DNA they would pass  on to their children for example good  question come on next question here is  some cancers are caused by viruses would  that be detected by genetic analysis  yeah you know this this is an astute  question that's a great question  there are some cancers that are caused  by viruses what an example would be  cervical cancer and human papillomavirus  HPV is also considered the causative  agent in some forms of head and neck  cancer and in fact these two examples  are the exact reason there's a strong  push now for HPV vaccinations in  children of certain ages  but we're finding that some of these  virally driven cancers respond extremely  well to certain treatments those can be  the virally driven cancers can sometimes  be identified by economic analysis many  of the genomic tests that are on the  market now interrogate a panel of genes  the inter-mountain precision genomics  ICG 100 tests and is an example of that  where we test a panel of genes testing a  panel of genes doesn't necessarily  detect inserted genomic DNA and nor do  other similar tests that are on the  market so I guess the short answer is  sometimes we can identify virally driven  tumors based on genomic analysis but not  every time right here a next question is  despite many cutting-edge technologies  why do people still die of cancers is it  probable or possible that environmental  factors distribute or disrupt early  diagnostics and treatment yeah great  question undoubtedly  there are environmental factors that  play significant roles in cancer not  only in their cause but in delaying  their diagnosis you know the best  examples of the causative environmental  factors of course include cigarette  smoking and you know exposure repeated  in prolonged sun exposure contributing  to melanoma and other hazardous  environmental agents contributing to  bladder cancers and other cancers in  terms of whether environmental agents  directly lead to delayed diagnosis it's  difficult to determine but it's clear  that our precision medicine technologies  are exciting and yielding new treatment  options but just as the questioner  pointed out they are not necessarily  resulting in new cures that we weren't  seeing before to date we've primarily  used precision medicine approaches  in patients with advanced or refractory  cancers and while we're seeing a  prolongation of life in those patients  we're not seeing cures and what we think  that means is we might be on the verge  of turning cancer into a chronic disease  which would be excellent for patients  given that in the past patients  diagnosed with cancer too frequently  have had their disease advanced and take  their lives in a matter of months and if  we could change that narrative so that  patients are living years or decades  with their disease that would be  exciting that's of course what we've  seen with diabetes or heart disease we  don't necessarily care patients of their  diabetes we don't cure patients of their  heart disease but we have treatments in  place where they can live normal lives  for prolonged periods with it with their  chronic conditions good question  thank you very much doctor and what  patient population do you treat i myself  have been treating patients with  advanced refractory cancer is looking  for just as i've been talking about in  this presentation looking for genomic  actionable alterations and providing  targeted therapies historically my focus  has been patients with gastrointestinal  malignancies path Intermountain and  healthcare  we've been treating all facets of  patients we've primarily focused our  precision medicine efforts on patients  with solid tumors but we treat patients  with both solid and liquid tumors  included including what follows Keith  good question all right here it looks  like a final question that we have time  for today is a rule patients insurance  cover the cost of genomic testing we  have found that insurance is covering  this and different payers cover at  different levels and different rates  our approach Intermountain Healthcare as  our not-for-profit organization has been  to work directly with payers so that  patients are not experiencing  out-of-pocket expenses and that's been  very successful and we're probably  we don't have patients experiencing  out-of-pocket expenses when this type of  testing is performed we anticipate that  this will continue to be the case and  we're excited that we are able to offer  that to patients thank you and do you  have any closing remarks for today's  presentation well I appreciate the  opportunity and for those who tuned in  thank you I know that everyone has busy  schedules and I appreciate the  opportunity to share with others  the experience that we've had is we've  tried to help patients by taking novel  and innovative approaches to treating  cancer our hope is that in the future  we'll continue to find cancer treatments  that are effective and yield prolong  survivals and maybe cures while not  increasing the cost of health care in  this country so I appreciate the  opportunity to present thank you for  your attention  I thank you and I like to thank the  doctor again for his wonderful  presentation I also like to thank the  audience for your outstanding questions  I'd also like to thank our sponsors  Intermountain precision genomics for  making today's educational webcast  possible today's webcast will be  available for on-demand viewing through  January of 2017 you'll receive an email  from lab roots alerting you when this  webcast will be available for replay we  invite you to forward that announcement  to your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event until next time  goodbye
 

